{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>To humble you and to test you, to do you good in your end.</b> All the humbling of the speaking being — the great toil that Israel had in the wilderness — was only to bring them to the trial, to habituate their nature in the trait of trust (bitaḥon) and to instill faith in HaShem, blessed be He, in their hearts, until the practice of divine service would return to them as nature, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 25:5): ״הדריכני באמתך ולמדני״ — from the language of (Jeremiah 2:24) ״פרא למוד מדבר״, and similarly (Hosea 10:11) ״עגלה מלומדה״. For David, peace be upon him, asked that the Omnipresent guide him in His truth until the act of His service would return to him as pure nature; and this is the language of the Torah (Deuteronomy 14:23): ״למען תלמד ליראה את ה' אלהיך״ — that is, that you become practiced and habituate your nature in the service of the Omnipresent until you need not exert yourself over it, and the act of awe become a natural thing for you to do, like the other natural things that you do by nature. And the fact that Scripture called the eating of the manna 'humbling' and said 'to humble you' comes to teach that anyone who has no food before him for a long time but only day by day — that eating is humbling for him, since he does not see the food before him. And likewise anyone who does not see what he eats is not satisfied; from here is derived the ruling for the blind, who eat and are not satisfied. And they brought as proof (Ecclesiastes 6:9): ״טוב מראה עינים מהלך נפש״. And because they did not see the food with their eyes, He called that eating 'humbling'."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:01.335560111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}